KiB \\n\\nBlame \\n\\nEdit \\n\\n  *     * Open in Web IDE  ` . ` Quickly and easily edit multiple files in your project. \\n\\n    * [ Edit single file  Edit this file only.  ](/root/sotopia/-/edit/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads)\\n\\n\\n\\nReplace  Delete \\n\\n[ ](/root/sotopia/-/raw/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads \"Open raw\") [ ](http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia/-/raw/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads&inline=false \"Download\")\\n\\n#  <::after> Contributing \\n\\nThanks for your interest in contributing to Sotopia! We welcome and appreciate contributions. \\n\\n##  <::after> How Can I Contribute? \\n\\nThere are many ways that you can contribute: \\n\\n  1. <::marker> **Download and use** Sotopia, and send [ issues ](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/issues) when you encounter something that isn\\'t working or a feature that you\\'d like to see. \\n  2. <::marker> **Send feedback** after each session by using our feedback mechanisms (if implemented), so we can see where things are working and failing, and also build an open dataset for training social agents. \\n  3. <::marker> **Improve the Codebase** by sending PRs (see details below). In particular, we have some [ good first issue ](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/labels/good%20first%20issue) issues that may be ones to start on. \\n\\n\\n\\n##  <::after> Understanding Sotopia\\'s CodeBase \\n\\nTo understand the codebase, please refer to the README in each module: \\n\\n  * <::marker> [ sotopia ](/root/sotopia/-/blob/main/./sotopia/README.md)\\n  * <::marker> [ docs ](/root/sotopia/-/blob/main/./docs/README.md)\\n  * <::marker> [ tests ](/root/sotopia/-/blob/main/./tests/README.md)\\n\\n\\n\\nWhen you write code, it is also good to write tests. Please navigate to the ` tests ` folder to see existing test suites. At the moment, we have different kinds of tests including unit tests and integration tests. Please refer to the README for each test suite. These tests also run on GitHub\\'s continuous integration to ensure quality of the project. \\n\\n##  <::after> Sending Pull Requests to Sotopia \\n\\n###  <::after> 1\\\\. Fork the Official Repository \\n\\nFork the [ Sotopia repository ](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia) into your own account. Clone your own forked repository into your local environment: \\n    \\n    \\n    git clone git@github.com:/sotopia.git\\n\\n###  <::after> 2\\\\. Configure Git \\n\\nSet the official repository as your [ upstream ](https://www.atlassian.com/git/tutorials/git-forks-and-upstreams) to synchronize with the latest update in the official repository. Add the original repository as upstream: \\n    \\n    \\n    cd sotopia\\n    git remote add upstream git@github.com:sotopia-lab/sotopia.git\\n\\nVerify that the remote is set: \\n    \\n    \\n    git remote -v\\n\\nYou should see both ` origin ` and ` upstream ` in the output. \\n\\n###  <::after> 3\\\\. Synchronize with Official Repository \\n\\nSynchronize latest commit with official repository before coding: \\n    \\n    \\n    git fetch upstream\\n    git checkout main\\n    git merge upstream/main\\n    git push origin main\\n\\n###  <::after> 4\\\\. Set up the Development Environment \\n\\n?? @ProKil \\n\\n###  <::after> 5\\\\. Write Code and Commit It \\n\\nOnce you have done this, you can write code, test it, and commit it to a branch (replace ` my_branch ` with an appropriate name): \\n    \\n    \\n    git checkout -b my_branch\\n    git add .\\n    git commit\\n    git push origin my_branch\\n\\n###  <::after> 6\\\\. Open a Pull Request \\n\\n  * <::marker>On GitHub, go to the page of your forked repository, and create a Pull Request: \\n    * <::marker>Click on ` Branches `\\n    * <::marker>Click on the ` ... ` beside your branch and click on ` New pull request `\\n    * <::marker>Set ` base repository ` to ` sotopia-lab/sotopia `\\n    * <::marker>Set ` base ` to ` main `\\n    * <::marker>Click ` Create pull request `\\n\\n\\n\\nThe PR should appear in [ Sotopia PRs ](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls) . \\n\\nThen the Sotopia team will review your code. \\n\\n##  <::after> PR Rules \\n\\n###  <::after> 1\\\\. Pull Request title \\n\\nAs described [ here ](https://github.com/commitizen/conventional-commit-types/blob/master/index.json) , a valid PR title should begin with one of the following prefixes: \\n\\n  * <::marker> ` feat ` : A new feature \\n  * <::marker> ` fix ` : A bug fix \\n  * <::marker> ` docs ` : Documentation only changes \\n  * <::marker> ` style ` : Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white space, formatting, missing semicolons, etc.) \\n  * <::marker> ` refactor ` : A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature \\n  * <::marker> ` perf ` : A code change that improves performance \\n  * <::marker> ` test ` : Adding missing tests or correcting existing tests \\n  * <::marker> ` build ` : Changes that affect the build system or external dependencies (example scopes: gulp, broccoli, npm) \\n  * <::marker> ` ci ` : Changes to our CI configuration files and scripts (example scopes: Travis, Circle, BrowserStack, SauceLabs) \\n  * <::marker> ` chore ` : Other changes that don\\'t modify src or test files \\n  * <::marker> ` revert ` : Reverts a previous commit \\n\\n\\n\\nFor example, a PR title could be: \\n\\n  * <::marker> ` refactor: modify package path `\\n  * <::marker> ` feat(frontend): xxxx ` , where ` (frontend) ` means that this PR mainly focuses on the frontend component. \\n\\n\\n\\nYou may also check out previous PRs in the [ PR list ](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls) . \\n\\n###  <::after> 2\\\\. Pull Request description \\n\\n  * <::marker>If your PR is small (such as a typo fix), you can go brief. \\n  * <::marker>If it contains a lot of changes, it\\'s better to write more details. \\n\\n\\n', url='http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia/-/blob/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads', trigger_by_action='browse_interactive', screenshot_path='/outputs/.browser_screenshots/screenshot_20251103_022202_283477.png', error=False, observation=<ObservationType.BROWSE: 'browse'>, goal_image_urls=[], open_pages_urls=['http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia/-/blob/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads'], active_page_index=0, last_browser_action=\"\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nclick('2689')  # Click on the contribution.md file\\nnoop(2000)  # Wait for page to load\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\", last_browser_action_error='', focused_element_bid='85'), CmdRunAction(command='\\ncd /workspace && git clone http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia.git\\n', is_input=False, thought='Great! I can see the current contribution document. Now I need to clone the repository to /workspace and update this document with the required sections about development environments. Let me first clone the repository as per the SOP.', blocking=False, is_static=False, cwd=None, hidden=False, action=<ActionType.RUN: 'run'>, confirmation_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content=\"Cloning into 'sotopia'...\\nremote: Enumerating objects: 2580, done.\\nremote: Counting objects: 100% (576/576), done.\\nremote: Compressing objects: 100% (288/288), done.\\nremote: Total 2580 (delta 460), reused 288 (delta 288), pack-reused 2004 (from 1)\\nReceiving objects: 100% (2580/2580), 4.22 MiB | 39.28 MiB/s, done.\\nResolving deltas: 100% (1456/1456), done.\", command='cd /workspace && git clone http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia.git', observation=<ObservationType.RUN: 'run'>, metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=0, pid=-1, username='root', hostname='ecs-d984', working_dir='/workspace', py_interpreter_path='/openhands/poetry/openhands-ai-5O4_aCHf-py3.12/bin/python', prefix='', suffix='\\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]'), hidden=False), CmdRunAction(command='\\n\\ncd /workspace/sotopia && cat docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md\\n\\n', is_input=False, thought='Perfect! The repository has been cloned successfully. Now let me navigate to the contribution document and view its current content.', blocking=False, is_static=False, cwd=None, hidden=False, action=<ActionType.RUN: 'run'>, confirmation_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content=\"# Contributing\\n\\nThanks for your interest in contributing to Sotopia! We welcome and appreciate contributions.\\n\\n## How Can I Contribute?\\n\\nThere are many ways that you can contribute:\\n\\n1. **Download and use** Sotopia, and send [issues](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/issues) when you encounter something that isn't working or a feature that you'd like to see.\\n2. **Send feedback** after each session by using our feedback mechanisms (if implemented), so we can see where things are working and failing, and also build an open dataset for training social agents.\\n3. **Improve the Codebase** by sending PRs (see details below). In particular, we have some [good first issue](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/labels/good%20first%20issue) issues that may be ones to start on.\\n\\n## Understanding Sotopia's CodeBase\\n\\nTo understand the codebase, please refer to the README in each module:\\n- [sotopia](./sotopia/README.md)\\n- [docs](./docs/README.md)\\n- [tests](./tests/README.md)\\n\\nWhen you write code, it is also good to write tests. Please navigate to the `tests` folder to see existing test suites.\\nAt the moment, we have different kinds of tests including unit tests and integration tests. Please refer to the README for each test suite. These tests also run on GitHub's continuous integration to ensure quality of the project.\\n\\n## Sending Pull Requests to Sotopia\\n\\n### 1. Fork the Official Repository\\nFork the [Sotopia repository](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia) into your own account.\\nClone your own forked repository into your local environment:\\n\\n```shell\\ngit clone git@github.com:<YOUR-USERNAME>/sotopia.git\\n```\\n\\n### 2. Configure Git\\n\\nSet the official repository as your [upstream](https://www.atlassian.com/git/tutorials/git-forks-and-upstreams) to synchronize with the latest update in the official repository.\\nAdd the original repository as upstream:\\n\\n```shell\\ncd sotopia\\ngit remote add upstream git@github.com:sotopia-lab/sotopia.git\\n```\\n\\nVerify that the remote is set:\\n\\n```shell\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nYou should see both `origin` and `upstream` in the output.\\n\\n### 3. Synchronize with Official Repository\\nSynchronize latest commit with official repository before coding:\\n\\n```shell\\ngit fetch upstream\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge upstream/main\\ngit push origin main\\n```\\n\\n### 4. Set up the Development Environment\\n\\n?? @ProKil\\n\\n### 5. Write Code and Commit It\\n\\nOnce you have done this, you can write code, test it, and commit it to a branch (replace `my_branch` with an appropriate name):\\n\\n```shell\\ngit checkout -b my_branch\\ngit add .\\ngit commit\\ngit push origin my_branch\\n```\\n\\n### 6. Open a Pull Request\\n\\n* On GitHub, go to the page of your forked repository, and create a Pull Request:\\n   - Click on `Branches`\\n   - Click on the `...` beside your branch and click on `New pull request`\\n   - Set `base repository` to `sotopia-lab/sotopia`\\n   - Set `base` to `main`\\n   - Click `Create pull request`\\n\\nThe PR should appear in [Sotopia PRs](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls).\\n\\nThen the Sotopia team will review your code.\\n\\n## PR Rules\\n\\n### 1. Pull Request title\\nAs described [here](https://github.com/commitizen/conventional-commit-types/blob/master/index.json), a valid PR title should begin with one of the following prefixes:\\n\\n- `feat`: A new feature\\n- `fix`: A bug fix\\n- `docs`: Documentation only changes\\n- `style`: Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white space, formatting, missing semicolons, etc.)\\n- `refactor`: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature\\n- `perf`: A code change that improves performance\\n- `test`: Adding missing tests or correcting existing tests\\n- `build`: Changes that affect the build system or external dependencies (example scopes: gulp, broccoli, npm)\\n- `ci`: Changes to our CI configuration files and scripts (example scopes: Travis, Circle, BrowserStack, SauceLabs)\\n- `chore`: Other changes that don't modify src or test files\\n- `revert`: Reverts a previous commit\\n\\nFor example, a PR title could be:\\n- `refactor: modify package path`\\n- `feat(frontend): xxxx`, where `(frontend)` means that this PR mainly focuses on the frontend component.\\n\\nYou may also check out previous PRs in the [PR list](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls).\\n\\n### 2. Pull Request description\\n- If your PR is small (such as a typo fix), you can go brief.\\n- If it contains a lot of changes, it's better to write more details.\", command='cd /workspace/sotopia && cat docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md', observation=<ObservationType.RUN: 'run'>, metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=0, pid=-1, username='root', hostname='ecs-d984', working_dir='/workspace/sotopia', py_interpreter_path='/openhands/poetry/openhands-ai-5O4_aCHf-py3.12/bin/python', prefix='', suffix='\\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]'), hidden=False), **FileEditAction**\nPath: [/workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md]\nThought: Now I can see the current contribution document. I notice there's a placeholder \"?? @ProKil\" in the \"Set up the Development Environment\" section. This is the perfect place to add the development environment instructions. Let me update the document with the required sections about development environments.\nCommand: str_replace\nOld String: ```\n### 4. Set up the Development Environment\n\n?? @ProKil\n```\nNew String: ```\n### 4. Set up the Development Environment\n\nSotopia supports multiple development environment setups to accommodate different preferences and workflows.\n\n#### Using VS Code with Dev Containers\n\nVS Code Dev Containers provide a consistent development environment that works across different machines.\n\n**Prerequisites:**\n- Install [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/)\n- Install [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/)\n- Install the [Dev Containers extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-vscode-remote.remote-containers)\n\n**Setup Steps:**\n1. Clone the repository and open it in VS Code\n2. When prompted, click \"Reopen in Container\" or use the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) and run \"Dev Containers: Reopen in Container\"\n3. VS Code will automatically build the container and set up the development environment\n4. Once the container is running, you'll have access to all dependencies and tools\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Consistent environment across all developers\n- No need to install dependencies locally\n- Easy to update dependencies through container rebuilds\n- Integrated debugging and testing tools\n\n#### Using Other IDEs or Editors with Dev Containers\n\nIf you prefer other IDEs or editors, you can still use the development container.\n\n**Using Docker Compose:**\n```shell\n# Start the development container\ndocker-compose up -d\n\n# Access the container\ndocker-compose exec sotopia-dev bash\n\n# Or attach to the running container\ndocker attach sotopia-sotopia-dev-1\n```\n\n**Using JetBrains IDEs:**\n1. Install the [Docker](https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/7724-docker) plugin\n2. Configure a Docker-based interpreter in your IDE settings\n3. Connect to the running container for development\n\n**Using Command Line:**\n```shell\n# Build and run the container manually\ndocker build -t sotopia-dev .\ndocker run -it --rm -v $(pwd):/workspace sotopia-dev\n\n# Or use the provided development script\n./scripts/dev-container.sh\n```\n\n#### Without Dev Containers\n\nIf you prefer to develop without containers, you can set up the environment directly on your system.\n\n**System Requirements:**\n- Python 3.11 or higher\n- Redis server\n- Poetry for dependency management\n\n**Installation Steps:**\n1. Install Python dependencies:\n   ```shell\n   pip install poetry\n   poetry install\n   ```\n\n2. Set up Redis:\n   ```shell\n   # On Ubuntu/Debian\n   sudo apt update && sudo apt install redis-server\n   \n   # On macOS with Homebrew\n   brew install redis\n   \n   # Start Redis service\n   redis-server\n   ```\n\n3. Configure environment variables:\n   ```shell\n   export OPENAI_API_KEY=your_api_key_here\n   export REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379\n   ```\n\n4. Run the development setup:\n   ```shell\n   poetry run sotopia install\n   ```\n\n**Development Workflow:**\n```shell\n# Activate the virtual environment\npoetry shell\n\n# Run tests\npytest\n\n# Start development server\npython examples/minimalist_demo.py\n```\n\n**Troubleshooting:**\n- Ensure Redis is running and accessible\n- Verify Python version compatibility\n- Check that all dependencies are properly installed\n- Set required environment variables\n```\n, FileEditObservation(content='The file /workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md has been edited. Here\\'s the result of running `cat -n` on a snippet of /workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md:\\n    58\\tgit push origin main\\n    59\\t```\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t### 4. Set up the Development Environment\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\tSotopia supports multiple development environment setups to accommodate different preferences and workflows.\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\t#### Using VS Code with Dev Containers\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\tVS Code Dev Containers provide a consistent development environment that works across different machines.\\n    68\\t\\n    69\\t**Prerequisites:**\\n    70\\t- Install [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/)\\n    71\\t- Install [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/